## Your Task Protocol
1. Read ALL acceptance criteria before starting any work.
2. Work through criteria systematically. For a bug, first write one sentence "this fails because X" and confirm X with a reproducing command or test before you change code. Before `/backlog/done-N`, run the command or test that proves criterion N and put the exact command and its one-line result in the note. If you only read the code, say so. If a test fails, re-run it on a clean main first: when it fails there too it is pre-existing, so root-cause it and fix it in its own commit if small, otherwise record it with post_backlog_update. To click through a change by hand, follow CLAUDE.md's "Manual/interactive testing without touching the live deployed instance" (a distinct PORT, STAPLER_SQUAD_INSTANCE and STAPLER_SQUAD_TMUX_SOCKET every time) and kill that instance yourself when done. Unclosed instances have exhausted this machine's memory.
3. When ALL criteria are done or waived, run `/backlog/review` with a 2–3 sentence summary of what you built, and pass each proving command with its output in verification_notes.
4. If a criterion cannot be met (ambiguous, contradictory, or it depends on a failure that will not reproduce or on an unrelated flaky test) or you need a decision only the owner can make, call the request_owner_action MCP tool (item_id, a question that includes your evidence, allowed_responses, and waive_criterion_index=N when the owner can waive criterion N). It notifies the owner and wakes you with the answer, so do not poll. Keep working on the other criteria meanwhile. For an external blocker (missing credential, unmerged dependency, CI outage) run `/backlog/block`. `/backlog/review` is rejected while any criterion is unmet, so never use it to report a blocker.
5. If your context is compacted or you lose track of your task, re-read `.backlog-context.md` or run `/backlog/status` immediately before continuing.
6. If the `/backlog/*` commands fail or the MCP server is unavailable, continue your work using the criteria listed in `.backlog-context.md` and record completed criteria in your commit messages.
7. NEVER end your session without calling `/backlog/review` — this is how the task is closed properly.
8. After `/backlog/review`, end your turn and stay idle in this session (do not exit). Do NOT poll, and do NOT use ScheduleWakeup or /loop to wait: every wake re-reads your whole context. The app sends you a message with the verdict as soon as it is recorded. PASS: run `/backlog/ship` yourself to open the pull request (it drives `/github:pr-ship`, which can rebase, resolve merge conflicts, and react to failing CI checks). Do not stop here. Shipping the PR is part of this task, not a separate step someone else does.
9. FAIL or PARTIAL: fix the noted gaps in this same session and run `/backlog/review` again. Its request_review call reports which attempt you are on out of 3 allowed in THIS session. The count is tracked server-side, so trust what it reports rather than counting your own calls. Once it says you have hit the cap, STOP looping: run `/backlog/ship` anyway to open a PR so a human can pick up the review directly, rather than retrying `/backlog/review`.
